    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The first recorded meeting between Teotihuacan and the Maya took place at Waka' in January 378, when the war leader Siyaj K'ahk' passed through roughly eight to ten days before taking over Tikal: the moment archaeologists call La Entrada.</li><li>Waka' abandoned its early alliance with Tikal for Calakmul, sealed by the marriage of K'inich Balam to a princess of the Kan dynasty who bore the high title ix kaloomté; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lady-kabel">Lady K'abel</a> governed the Wak kingdom for the Snake dynasty between 672 and 692.</li><li>Tikal's ruler Yik'in Chan K'awiil defeated Waka' in 743, made it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> and carried off an image of a celestial serpent, possibly its patron deity; a decade later the city was destroyed and K'inich Balam's successor captured and sacrificed.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Burial 39, the tomb of an early seventh-century king, held 23 ceramic figurines of a royal court at a ceremony, among them a kneeling king with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">Maize</a> God's hair, a female singer conjuring a deer spirit, a dwarf blowing a shell trumpet and two helmeted dwarfs who may be boxers.</p></aside>
    </section>

